      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "Note"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "4a - HiRes COMPOSITE: put the ORIGINAL 8K panorama's texture back into the WAN frames\n\nWHY\nWAN repaints every frame, so the real texture of your panorama is replaced by generated\napproximation -- and differently in every frame. A Gaussian splat has to explain every view\nwith ONE 3D model, so whatever the views disagree about comes out as blur. But the original\npanorama still exists at 8192x4096 and the camera rail is known, so the correct pixel is\nKNOWABLE: reproject the original through the same MoGe geometry and read it off. WAN is then\nonly needed where geometry has no answer (the disocclusion holes).\n\nMeasured against the shipping pipeline, same scenes and settings:\n  eval PSNR          +1.31 dB indoor / +2.37 dB outdoor\n  detail retained    31.9% -> 57.5% indoor,  25.4% -> 61.4% outdoor  (= +115% / +338%)\n  SfM pose residual  0.09% -> 0.04%  /  0.14% -> 0.02%\n  multi-view agreement of frames that share a pose:  38.6 -> 45.8 dB\n\nWHAT YOU NEED FIRST (from workflow 1)\n  * the WAN video for each trajectory\n  * that trajectory's camera rail, at\n      <ComfyUI>/output/<project>/_work/camplot_rail_<output_name>_<node_id>.json\n    Camera Plot prints the exact path when it runs, and also returns it as a rail_json\n    OUTPUT you can wire straight across if you build this into one graph.\n\n    ONLY runs made with this version of the pack have those files. The plain\n    camplot_rail.json is SHARED: every Camera Plot node writing to the same project\n    overwrites it, so in an older multi-trajectory run only the LAST rail survived and the\n    others are gone for good -- nothing else on disk records the path a clip was flown\n    along. Those older scenes cannot be composited without re-running Camera Plot.\n\nWIRING\n  1. 8K SOURCE PANORAMA  -> panorama_hires. Put the full-resolution pano in ComfyUI/input.\n     This is the whole point of the workflow: a 2K image here buys nothing.\n  2. CONDITIONING PANORAMA -> panorama_geometry. The SAME image you fed Camera Plot.\n     Depth comes from this one so the reprojection lines up with what WAN generated.\n     A different copy (an HDR tonemap, a re-upscale) misaligns it -- measured coverage\n     collapsed 0.51 -> 0.04.\n  3. One WAN clip per trajectory -> wan_frames. Set 'video' on each loader.\n  4. Paste that trajectory's rail path into 'rail'. Give each node its own traj_index\n     (0,1,2,3) and the SAME set_name -- they share one frames/ folder, and putting every\n     trajectory into ONE reconstruction is what supplies the real 3D parallax.\n  5. Trajectories 1-3 ship MUTED so the graph runs with only trajectory 0 filled in.\n     Un-mute a pair (right-click -> Mode -> Always) as you add each further clip. Four\n     trajectories from one anchor is the measured configuration.\n\nTHE THREE SETTINGS THAT MATTER (each measured, not guessed)\n  base_mode    = geometry   the source IS the image, WAN only fills holes. Biggest single\n                            quality change in the whole project.\n  output_width = 8192       set it to your SOURCE panorama's width. At 8192 one output pixel\n                            covers one source pixel, so the 8K is sampled 1:1 through mip 0\n                            instead of being downsampled first (+26% reconstructed detail).\n  frames       = 0-80/2     41 of 81, evenly spaced. Set the SAME value on every composite.\n\nFRAME DENSITY, measured on a 5090 at 8192 wide, 4 trajectories (3.6 s/frame steady state):\n  0-80/2  (default)  41 frames   14 min   3.8 GB   even along the whole path\n  all                81 frames   23 min   7.4 GB   adjacent frames overlap heavily, so mostly\n                                                   redundancy -- and SfM matches every pair,\n                                                   so it is ~10x the matching work\n  0-15,16-/8         25 frames   10 min   2.3 GB   cheapest, but the tail of each path goes\n                                                   sparse and that is where a splat is weakest\nPeak ~11 GB VRAM. Drop output_width to 4096 to halve the time while you test -- but only if\nyour source pano is 4096 too, or the source gets rejected as minified.\n\nOUTPUT\n  <ComfyUI>/output/hires_composite/frames/   the 8K composites (what the splat trains on)\n  <ComfyUI>/output/hires_composite/proxies/  the same frames at proxy_width (what SfM poses on)\nThen run 4b_hires_composite_to_dataset.json."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
